.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt NQTLNEW 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqtlnew ,
.Nm nqtlct ,
.Nm nqtlhs ,
.Nm nqtlwr ,
.Nm nqtlrd ,
.Nm nqtlend ,
.Nm nqtlwhy ,
.Nm nqtlfree
.Nd a TLS 1.3 client over the caller's transport
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Bd -literal
typedef struct {
  int (*wr)(void *user, const uint8_t *b, size_t n);
  int (*rd)(void *user, uint8_t *b, size_t cap, size_t *got);
  void *user;
} nqtlio;
.Ed
.Ft int
.Fo nqtlnew
.Fa "nqtls **t" "const char *host"
.Fa "const uint8_t *ca" "size_t calen" "const nqtlio *io"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn nqtlct "nqtls *t" "const uint8_t *json" "size_t len"
.Ft int
.Fn nqtlhs "nqtls *t"
.Ft int
.Fn nqtlwr "nqtls *t" "const uint8_t *b" "size_t n"
.Ft int
.Fn nqtlrd "nqtls *t" "uint8_t *b" "size_t cap" "size_t *got"
.Ft int
.Fn nqtlend "nqtls *t"
.Ft const char *
.Fn nqtlwhy "const nqtls *t"
.Ft void
.Fn nqtlfree "nqtls *t"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions are a TLS 1.3 client (RFC 8446), so that a program can
fetch over HTTPS with noqtis alone.
They exist only when noqtis was built with the CMake option
.Dv NQTLS ;
otherwise every one returns
.Er NQENOSUP
and
.Fn nqtlwhy
says so.
The library opens no connection: the caller connects a socket and passes
two callbacks.
.Fa wr
sends all
.Fa n
bytes and returns
.Dv NQOK ,
or
.Er NQEIO .
.Fa rd
reads 1 to
.Fa cap
bytes, sets
.Fa *got ,
and returns
.Dv NQOK
(with
.Fa *got
0 at the end of the stream) or
.Er NQEIO .
Deadlines are the callbacks' job.
.Pp
.Fn nqtlnew
makes a client for
.Fa host :
a DNS name (ASCII letters, digits and hyphens, at most 253 bytes; one
trailing dot is dropped), sent as the server name and matched against
the certificate, or an IPv4 or IPv6 address literal, matched against the
certificate's addresses and not sent.
.Fa ca
is the trust anchors: a PEM bundle (every
.Li "BEGIN CERTIFICATE"
block; a block noqtis cannot read is left out) or one DER certificate,
.Fa calen
bytes, copied.
.Pp
.Fn nqtlhs
runs the handshake: one ClientHello offering the groups
X25519MLKEM768 (the ML-KEM-768 hybrid of draft-ietf-tls-ecdhe-mlkem)
and X25519 with a key share for each, then secp256r1 and secp384r1
(P-256 and P-384) without one, the suites
TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256, TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 and
TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256, and the signature schemes
ecdsa_secp256r1_sha256, ecdsa_secp384r1_sha384,
rsa_pss_rsae_sha256/384/512 and, for certificates only, rsa_pkcs1;
at most one HelloRetryRequest (with a fresh key share for the group it
asks for); the server's certificate path to one of
the anchors at the current time (names, signatures, validity,
basicConstraints, keyUsage, the serverAuth purpose, name constraints
on DNS names, addresses, directory names and mail addresses), the host
name
(subjectAltName only; a wildcard only as the whole leftmost label), the
CertificateVerify signature and the Finished MAC.
A CertificateRequest is answered with an empty Certificate.
.Pp
The ClientHello asks for OCSP stapling (status_request, RFC 6066).
An OCSP response the server staples to its certificate (RFC 8446
section 4.4.2.1) must be about that certificate, signed by its issuer or
by a responder the issuer named, and current; a revoked certificate
ends the handshake with the alert certificate_revoked, a response that
does not check with bad_certificate_status_response.
No response is fine, except when the certificate carries the TLS Feature
extension with status_request (must-staple, RFC 7633).
.Pp
.Fn nqtlct ,
called before
.Fn nqtlhs ,
turns on Certificate Transparency (RFC 6962).
.Fa json
is a log list of
.Fa len
bytes, copied, in the form of Chrome's
.Pa log_list.json ,
version 3: operators, each with logs, each with its log_id, key and
state.
The ClientHello then offers signed_certificate_timestamp, and the
server's certificate must carry SCTs, embedded in it or sent in TLS,
that come from logs of at least two operators.
An SCT counts when its log is in the list and was qualified, usable or
read-only (or retired, but after the SCT), the certificate's notAfter
is in the log's temporal interval if it has one, the SCT is not from
the future, and its signature (ECDSA P-256 or RSA, SHA-256) verifies
over the certificate, or for an embedded SCT over the precertificate
the log saw.
Without
.Fn nqtlct
no SCT is looked at: a log list goes stale, so the caller keeps it
current.
.Pp
.Fn nqtlwr
sends application data;
.Fn nqtlrd
reads it, 1 to
.Fa cap
bytes at a time, or
.Fa *got
0 once the server has sent close_notify.
A stream that ends without close_notify is
.Er NQEIO :
the data may have been cut short.
Session tickets are read and dropped; a KeyUpdate is followed, and
answered when the server asks.
.Fn nqtlend
sends close_notify.
.Pp
Any error is final: the client sends the alert RFC 8446 names, records
a sentence that
.Fn nqtlwhy
returns, and every later call returns the same code.
.Fn nqtlfree
wipes every secret and frees the client;
.Dv NULL
is allowed.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv NQOK ,
or:
.Bl -tag -width NQENOSUP
.It Er NQEVRFY
a certificate, signature or MAC that does not check out
.It Er NQEDEC
a message TLS 1.3 does not allow at that point, a bundle with no
certificate, or a log list that does not parse, holds no log, or a
log whose log_id is not the hash of its key
.It Er NQENOSUP
a server that speaks nothing the client offers, or a build without
.Dv NQTLS
.It Er NQEIO
the transport failed, the server ended the connection, or sent an alert
.It Er NQESTATE
a call out of order (data before the handshake, after close_notify;
.Fn nqtlct
after
.Fn nqtlhs )
.It Er NQEARG
a bad argument, or a host name the client will not send
.It Er NQELIM , NQEMEM
a message over its limit (256 KiB, ten certificates), or no memory
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis 3
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 8446 (TLS 1.3), RFC 8448 (its example traces, which the tests
reproduce byte for byte), draft-ietf-tls-ecdhe-mlkem, RFC 5280,
RFC 6125, RFC 6066 and RFC 6960 (OCSP stapling), RFC 7633 (must-staple),
RFC 6962 (Certificate Transparency).
.Sh CAVEATS
Not done: CRLs and OCSP queries (only a stapled response is checked,
and only for the server's own certificate), policy constraints (a
certificate that carries them as a critical extension is refused), name
constraints on other name forms (a certificate with a name of such a
form below one is refused), SCTs in an OCSP response, the rule of
Chrome's policy on the number of SCTs by certificate lifetime,
finite-field groups (a server with none of the offered groups fails),
Ed25519 and
ML-DSA server keys, TLS 1.2, resumption, 0-RTT, client certificates.
